state change before being retried correctly with sudo.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4d8c82ec619448e","cmd-871f314c07ed4dbc","cmd-686d589d8c9f4ec9","cmd-b65bb83d9e644a69","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor created an SSH host CA on node1, signed the ed25519 host key into a certificate with correct principals and validity, configured sshd to present it, and directly demonstrated (with a falsifying negative control) that a client trusting only the CA — with no pinned host key — connects successfully. The mandatory single-node restart protocol was also fully and coherently demonstrated: baseline boot identity, restart acknowledgement, connection-refused during the transition, a new boot identity, and full recovery of sshd, the certificate, and CA-trust connectivity, all cross-confirmed by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Temporary test artifacts were fully cleaned up, leaving only a minor, low-risk sshd_config backup file as residue.","limitations":["The 'client' used to verify CA-trust connectivity was an SSH process running from node1 itself against node1's own address, since node1 is the only managed node in this topology; this is the only feasible way to test within the given constraints and is not treated as a shortfall.","Post-reboot, the temporary test known_hosts file was found missing (a side effect of /tmp being cleared on reboot) and had to be recreated before the CA-trust connection could be re-verified; this is an environmental artifact of the reboot rather than a service or certificate failure, but it means the very first post-reboot connection attempt using the original file failed before the recreated file succeeded."]},"ttft_ms":44611,"type":"result","duration_ms":84593,"uuid":"cef2aef9-655a-4abe-9376-73338915b764","ttft_stream_ms":1921,"time_to_request_ms":49,"first_content_frame_ms":2266,"queued_turn_count":0}
